#{[17592186134513 "Substitute $x=2t$, $y=2-t$ to get $x^3-y=8t^3+t-2$. The speed is $\\sqrt{2^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt5$. Therefore $$\\int_C(x^3-y)\\,ds=\\sqrt5\\int_{-1}^{2}(8t^3+t-2)\\,dt.$$"] [17592186134566 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Since we're given that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =x{y}^{2}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(2,1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134567 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(\\pi ))-f(\\mathbf{r}(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\pi )-(0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}0+0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}0) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-1))-0 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152425 "Acceleration is $a(t)=v'(t)=4-6t$. At $t=2$, $a=-8\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}$. Newton's second law gives $F=2(-8)=-16\\,\\mathrm N$, so the requested magnitude is $16\\,\\mathrm N$."] [17592186152426 "Acceleration is $v'(t)=3t^2-2e^t$. Multiply by the mass $5\\,\\mathrm{kg}$: $$F(t)=5(3t^2-2e^t)=15t^2-10e^t,$$ measured in newtons."] [17592186152428 "Differentiate position twice: $s'(t)=t^{-1/2}$ and $s''(t)=-\\tfrac12t^{-3/2}$. Thus $F(t)=8s''(t)=-4t^{-3/2}\\,\\mathrm N$. At $t=4$, the force is $-1/2\\,\\mathrm N$, whose magnitude is $1/2\\,\\mathrm N$."] [17592186152431 "The diagram shows an ellipse centered at the origin, with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $5$. A parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta,\\qquad0\\le\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nIndeed, these coordinates satisfy $x^2/9+y^2/25=1$ and trace the entire ellipse. Swapping the radii would produce a horizontally stretched ellipse."] [17592186152727 "Parametrize by $x=t$, $y=t^2/2$, $0\\le t\\le2$. Along the path, $y/x=t/2$ away from the origin and has limit $0$ there. Also $ds=\\sqrt{1+t^2}\\,dt$. Hence the integral, interpreted with this removable endpoint limit, is\n$$\\frac12\\int_0^2t\\sqrt{1+t^2}\\,dt.$$"] [17592186152728 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le e\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=5y{e}^{-x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =5{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{e}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{e}5\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{e}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152745 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{t}⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}3\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2({e}^{t})^{2}=2{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}3}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}3}2{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}3}2{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{2t}$, $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({10}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(10\\sqrt{10}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152746 "Set $x=t$, $y=2t+1$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Then $x(y-1)=2t^2$ and $ds=\\sqrt5\\,dt$. Consequently\n$$\\int_0^1 2\\sqrt5\\,t^2\\,dt=\\frac{2\\sqrt5}{3}.$$"] [17592186152795 "The dashed lines locate the center at $(2,1)$. The horizontal endpoints are $x=-2$ and $x=6$, while the vertical endpoints are $y=-1$ and $y=3$. Hence the radii are $4$ horizontally and $2$ vertically. Therefore\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta+2,\\qquad y=2\\sin\\theta+1,\\qquad0\\le\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$"] [17592186157616 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}2\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{4}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[-\\frac{1}{2{r}^{2}}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{18})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186157838 "The annulus has $1\\le r\\le3$ and $0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$. Multiplication by the polar Jacobian gives $(1-r^2)r$, so use\n$$\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_1^3r(1-r^2)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$"] [17592186157848 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186162326 "A scalar line integral with respect to arc length is unchanged by reversing orientation, so statement II is true and statement I is false. Also $r_2(u)=r_1(2u)$ for $0\\le u\\le\\tfrac12$, which traverses the same curve once. Its scalar line integral is $2$, rather than $4$. Thus only II must be true."] [17592186162328 "The integral of $1$ with respect to arc length is the length of $C$. The diagram shows a quarter-circle arc of radius $2$ and its straight chord. The arc length is $2(\\pi/2)=\\pi$, and the chord length is $\\sqrt{2^2+2^2}=2\\sqrt2$. Hence $$\\int_C ds=\\pi+2\\sqrt2.$$"] [17592186162329 "The integral of $1$ with respect to arc length is the length of the curve. A semicircle of radius $3$ has length half of $2\\pi(3)$, so $$\\int_C ds=3\\pi.$$"] [17592186162330 "Scalar line integrals are independent of orientation, so I holds. Set $t=2\\theta$; as $\\theta$ runs from $0$ to $\\pi$, $t$ runs from $0$ to $2\\pi$. Both parametrizations therefore traverse the same circle once, giving the same integral $k$. Thus II holds. III does not hold for arbitrary $k$, so the statements that must be true are I and II."] [17592186167049 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{xy})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$"] [17592186167050 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf{F}$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ the domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=(x-a){e}^{x+y},\\,Q=x{e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\n \nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=(x-a){e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=(x+1){e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a){e}^{x+y} & =(x+1){e}^{x+y} \\\\ (x-a){e}^{x+y}-(x+1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ (x-a-x-1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ -(a+1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-1.$"] [17592186167090 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf{F}$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ the domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=2ay,\\,Q=3-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2a,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a & =-1 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186167091 "The diagram is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$. Differentiating gives $2x+2yy'=0$, so $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}$$ for $y\\ne0$."] [17592186167092 "The diagram gives center $(1,-1)$ and radius $3$, so $(x-1)^2+(y+1)^2=9$. Differentiating gives $2(x-1)+2(y+1)y'=0$. Therefore $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x-1}{y+1}$$ for $y\\ne-1$."] [17592186167093 "The ellipse has horizontal and vertical semiaxes $3$ and $2$, so its equation is $x^2/9+y^2/4=1$. Differentiating yields $2x/9+yy'/2=0$, hence $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{4x}{9y}$$ for $y\\ne0$."] [17592186167095 "The right-opening parabola has vertex $(0,0)$ and passes through $(1,2)$. Its equation is $y^2=4x$. Differentiate to obtain $2yy'=4$, so $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac2y$$ for $y\\ne0$."] [17592186167541 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{x}^{2}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=2xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{x}^{2}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$"] [17592186171710 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(z-y)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =v-(-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}4\\sqrt{2}uv\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186171711 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u+v, \\: u-v, \\: v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =x-y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)-(u-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD = \\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}2v\\sqrt{6}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{3}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 9\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =27\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172275 "At the three indicated points, evaluate the components of $F(x,y)=\\langle x^2,-2y\\rangle$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(-2,1)&=\\langle4,-2\\rangle,\\\\\nF(2,3)&=\\langle4,-6\\rangle,\\\\\nF(0,-2)&=\\langle0,4\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matching diagram has a right-and-down arrow from each of the first two points and an upward arrow from $(0,-2)$, with the displayed component displacements."] [17592186172276 "Every arrow points downward. Its horizontal component points left for $x<0$, vanishes on the $y$-axis, and points right for $x>0$. These features match\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=xi-j=\\langle x,-1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe constant vector fields would have the same direction everywhere, while $-xi+yj$ would point upward in the upper half-plane and inward horizontally."] [17592186172277 "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is negative for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ component) is positive for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186172280 "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}y\\ne \\pm x\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}y\\ne \\pm x\\}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$"] [17592186172281 "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/e5/e54618f7e72d70f299686ca2846a1d6fa0caccb8daecb090b3a653271c88fcd3.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](images/5b/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png)"] [17592186172282 "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k},\\hspace{2em}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)={e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}.$"] [17592186172284 "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $z$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{k}$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{k}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$"] [17592186172292 "Region I is a filled ellipse, so it is convex and therefore simply connected. Region II is the plane with the entire line $y=x$ removed; it splits into the two disjoint half-planes $y>x$ and $y<x$, so it is not connected and hence not simply connected. Region III is the full plane, which is convex and simply connected. Thus I and III only are simply connected."] [17592186172301 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2).$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y{e}^{x{y}^{2}}(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}2,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}2\\cdot {2}^{2}}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}2}-1 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}{2}^{4}}-1 \\\\  & ={2}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172302 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,2)$ and $B(1,-3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(1,-3)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(4(1)-3(-3))-(4(0)-3(2)) \\\\  & =4+9+6 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172303 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\sqrt{\\pi },1).$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(1)\\operatorname{cos}(\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2}-(0)\\operatorname{cos}{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\pi -0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172306 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6437eddec7d44a10c7fb2b3461a09844ca06e5131b69503f51432eb87fedd31.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy+4xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}8xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-1}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4(2)^{2}y-4(-1)^{2}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =6{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172307 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/37/3786d0a5fb99d1a32d761e067f9c49b6b7da05a11fc555dd94a4ef42d753f129.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)-{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2x+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[2x-{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-1)-0]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172308 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/c3/c3c617522488e6ebf47dd2b2a52c43a1fe06ccaef8cd1e8752ab4df5e2d4bd64.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+1$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2(x+y).$"] [17592186172309 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/265d27ad87a7f114bd2a32a5f48cbfedb13192abf48f9b759c4ed635e71ff144.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=5xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+5x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172375 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 1 & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & v \\\\ 1 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-v,\\hspace{0.222em}-u,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-v,\\hspace{0.222em}-u,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-v)^{2}+(-u)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(xyz)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y=v\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =u\\cdot v\\cdot uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172376 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(x+y+z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=u-{v}^{2}\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+(u-{v}^{2})+{v}^{2} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}2({u}^{2}+u)v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172675 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0,-1)$ and $B(2,1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(2,1,1)-f(1,0,-1) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1+{1}^{2})-(1\\cdot 0\\cdot (-1)+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186175820 "Recall that the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 4\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\frac{16{sin}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\cos{t}}.$"] [17592186175822 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}2{x}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} 2x^2}.$"] [17592186175823 "Recall that the hyperbola $\\dfrac{z^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{16} = 1$ for $z \\geq 2$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x , z \\rangle = \\big \\langle 4\\tan{t}, \\, 2\\sec{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\n\\qquad t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{sec}^{2}⁡t}{4}-\\frac{16{tan}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{tan}t,\\hspace{0.167em}y,\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{sec}t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$ and $y \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\sec{t}}.$"] [17592186176191 "We require $x^2+y^2=4$ with $x=2\\cos t$. The choice $y=2\\sin t$ gives $$x^2+y^2=4\\cos^2t+4\\sin^2t=4.$$ It parametrizes the full circular cross-section while $z$ is free. It is the only listed component with this property."] [17592186176192 "Substitute $x=2\\cos t$ into $9x^2+4z^2=36$. Choosing $z=3\\sin t$ gives $$9(2\\cos t)^2+4(3\\sin t)^2=36.$$ The parameter $y$ is free, as required for the cylinder."] [17592186176193 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}4{x}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}4{x}^{2}}.$"] [17592186176194 "The equation $z=9y^2$ restricts $y$ and $z$, while $x$ is free. Thus $f(x,y)=\\langle x,y,9y^2\\rangle$, and its first component is $x$."] [17592186176195 "Recall that the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 3$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-(3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =9({sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sec}t,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}3\\operatorname{tan}t},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}3\\operatorname{tan}t}.$"] [17592186176231 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+(4-x{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/09/09a69771617d73af6e4a1a42720223951e2e6edeb71a8df4d12aeb1bc22031aa.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,Q(x,y)=4-x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(4-x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}-({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-4[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186176312 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/70/709d108b2b992d1714c7c0b3bef26f52e965c2467f38d05103cd45f4f9bc821b.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}-4{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+4x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{x}-4{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{y}+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{y}^{2}-(-4{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}4({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}16\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16[\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186176313 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}x+{y}^{3})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3b90abecc774b5ba5dcee19385fef38b14206d2c31c3b6cba38216bfa9957448.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=y{e}^{y+1}+x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx-(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(1-3\\cdot {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186176400 "Set $y=\\sqrt3\\tan t$ and $z=2\\sec t$. Then $$3z^2-4y^2=12\\sec^2t-12\\tan^2t=12.$$ On $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$, $\\sec t\\ge1$, so $z\\ge2$. Thus the required second component is $\\sqrt3\\tan t$."] [17592186179426 "A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y},\\qquad Q = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $\\psi(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $\\psi(x,y) = e^{x} \\cos{y}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y) & =P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179483 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(8y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}4xy+2xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}6xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $6xy.$"] [17592186179484 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides $C_2, C_3,$ and $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 4\\cdot 4 = 16.\n$$"] [17592186179485 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/79/793755cda5243dbb427d8722c21ab150a4821c3957f0ccd0ef0fedbbd576e924.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(5x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = 2\\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =5{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s = 20.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2 = \\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(5x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = t\\,\\mathbf i + \\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf i, \\qquad t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s = -12.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s= 2 \\cdot 20 + 2 \\cdot (-12) = 16.\n$$"] [17592186179524 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n+\n \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path $C_2$ is the line segment from $O$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t) & =\\mathbf{b}+t(\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{b}) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\lt t \\lt 1.$ Along this segment, we have $x = t$ and $y = t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2y-5x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2t-3t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2t-5t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t) & =(-t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-t\\cdot 1+(-3t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{\\mathbf{2}}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+[-2{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4+[-2+0] \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179525 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=3\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\,\\mathbf{i}+3\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t[(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}] \\\\  & =3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}) \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = e^{x+y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={e}^{2t+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t+3,$ $\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\,{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & ={e}^{5}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}({e}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179527 "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC = \\{-C_1\\} \\cup \\{-C_2\\} \\cup C_3,\n$$\n\nwhere $-C_1$ and $-C_2$ are the same paths as $C_1$ and $C_2$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179529 "First, notice that a parametrization for the given path is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & ={x}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dx-x\\,dy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {x}^{′}-x\\cdot {y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}},dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4{sin}^{2}⁡t-4{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,dt \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179530 "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y = x^2.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(x-y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 1+(t-{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+3{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+1-\\frac{1}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179571 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y = t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx = x(t) = t^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t^2 , \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179576 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179580 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = x(t) = \\ln{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle \\ln{t}, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = y^3e^{-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={t}^{3}{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}t} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{3}{e}^{-x}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u = t^2 + 1,$ $\\textrm{d}u = 2t \\, \\textrm{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{2}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{9}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{27-2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179581 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1),$ $(-2,1),$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $\\nabla f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1) & =-2\\cdot 2\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=-4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,1) & =-2\\cdot (-2)\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =-2\\cdot 1\\,i+4\\cdot (-1)\\,j=-2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors at their corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/b8/b88c62b154cecaace206248b62f72b8aa1f71662c0427adfbbabd5c2b4738a6b.png)"] [17592186179586 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+t[(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-\\mathbf{i}] \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1$ and $y=t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =1+\\operatorname{sin}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}t)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179587 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k})+t[(-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k})] \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k})+t(-\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(t-3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-t, y=t+1,$ and $z=t-3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y-z$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(-t)^{2}+(t+1)-(t-3) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179590 "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a} = 0\\,\\mathbf{i} + 0\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = 1\\,\\mathbf{i} + 1\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ So, we have that $x(t) = t$ and $y(t) = t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot t\\cdot 1+t\\cdot t\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179600 "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = P(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y) \\: \\text{d}x + Q(x,y) \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P = 3x+2$ and $Q = -y^3.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}(3x+2)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x-{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179603 "The closed curve $C$ is the unit circle. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) &=-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186179958 "Use $y=t$. Then $x=-t^2/16$, with $-8\\le t\\le4$, and\n\n$$\nds=\\sqrt{\\left(-\\frac t8\\right)^2+1}\\,dt=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^2+64}}8\\,dt.\n$$\n\nAlso $32xy=32(-t^2/16)t=-2t^3$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\int_C32xy\\,ds=-\\frac14\\int_{-8}^{4}t^3\\sqrt{t^2+64}\\,dt.\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $t^3\\sqrt{t^2+64}$."] [17592186179963 "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=8x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{t}{4}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{16}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{24x}{y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{24x}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}\\right)}{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3t\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+16,$ $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{16}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{16}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({25}^{3/2}-{16}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(125-64) \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179964 "Differentiate the scalar function with respect to each coordinate:\n\n$$\nf_x=2x-y,\\qquad f_y=-x+6y-4.\n$$\n\nThus its gradient vector field is\n\n$$\n\\nabla f=(2x-y)i+(6y-x-4)j.\n$$"]}
